<Layout title="The SAGE Anomaly — Origin Dispatch" description="How the SAGE Anomaly story began: James, his grandfather's fragmented memories of North Bay, and the anomaly recovered from SAGE training film and reconstructed with DCGAN and pix2pix.">
  <article class="dispatch">
    <div class="dispatch-hero">
      <img src="/images/operatorConsoleArchiveFrame.png" alt="Operators working at a SAGE-era console, recovered from the archive James brought in" />
      <div class="hero-fade"></div>
    </div>

    <div class="dispatch-inner">
      <div class="dispatch-header">
        <span class="tx-label">// FOUNDING DISPATCH</span>
        <span class="tx-coords">SAGE ANOMALY ORIGIN · NOV 2018</span>
        <h1>THE SAGE<br/><span class="h1-sub">ANOMALY</span></h1>
      </div>

      <div class="tx-meta">
        <div class="tx-line"><span class="tx-key">SOURCE</span><span class="tx-val">Originally published on Patreon, Nov 18, 2018</span></div>
        <div class="tx-line"><span class="tx-key">SUBJECT</span><span class="tx-val">How this project began — a family story, a box of SAGE archives, and the anomaly recovered from the film</span></div>
        <div class="tx-line"><span class="tx-key">FACILITY</span><span class="tx-val">North Bay SAGE Direction Center, 600 feet underground, North Bay, Ontario</span></div>
        <div class="tx-line"><span class="tx-key">FORMAT</span><span class="tx-val">Historical fiction short story, with crypto AI art</span></div>
      </div>

      <div class="dispatch-body terminal-block">
        <p>
          James came to me with a mystery — a computer mystery. His grandfather had served in a
          Canadian army unit responsible for operating a secret computer node in North Bay, Ontario.
          It was underground, and part of a massive — for its day — computer network called SAGE.
        </p>
        <p>
          Based on my study and research into the history of SAGE, he thought I might have an idea
          of what happened the night of January 15th, 1966. He came with a large box of manuals,
          rolls of film, computer tape reels, and punch tape rolls.
        </p>

        <figure>
          <img src="/images/southPortalNorthBay.png" alt="The south portal cut into the rock at North Bay" />
          <figcaption>The south portal</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <p>
          SAGE (Semi-Automatic Ground Environment) is a computer network that consisted of
          <a href="http://www.snarc.net/tr/sage.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener">56 IBM AN/FSQ-7/8 computers</a>
          scattered across the United States and Canada. There were about 24 Direction Centers with
          two computers each — if one failed, they would switch to the secondary computer. One of
          these centers was 600 feet underground in North Bay, Ontario.
        </p>

        <figure>
          <img src="/images/locationsSage.png" alt="Locations of the SAGE Computers across North America" />
          <figcaption>Locations of SAGE computers</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <p>
          His story was pieced together from multiple conversations with his grandfather as he
          drifted into Alzheimer's.
        </p>

        <blockquote>
          <p>"Damn it, why is tape T in the system again? Get those girly pictures off the screens."</p>
          <cite>— James's grandfather, as recalled by James</cite>
        </blockquote>

        <p>
          He always started out that way, James said. "Put the P tape back in. Why is the A side
          and B side both active? The system is overloaded."
        </p>

        <p>
          "James," I said, "Tape T was the test tape. It was used to run simulations of Russian
          bomber attacks, although there were test patterns on it also."
        </p>

        <p>
          James finally told me what he had pieced together. On January 15, 1966, he believed his
          grandfather had described a major anomaly. Random drawings appeared on the command center
          screen. They kept changing, becoming more advanced. He remembered because the night was
          cold — minus 35°C — and he'd been able to park in the tunnel that night.
        </p>

        <figure>
          <img src="/images/sageCommandCenterScreen.png" alt="SAGE combat center — operators at the command center console" />
          <figcaption>SAGE Combat Center — an operations room like the one James's grandfather worked in</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <blockquote>
          <p>"Shut it down now — remember in '59, when static on the modem line almost launched a CIM-10. Route the modems to New York until this interference stops."</p>
          <cite>— James's grandfather, as recalled by James</cite>
        </blockquote>

        <p>
          "Sometimes he would say the anomaly was caused by a programmer who liked to draw pictures
          on the screens. He thought someone had loaded the computer with images drawn using the
          light guns. But there are reports in this box that say it was looked into, and all they
          ever found was a single image of a pin-up girl," James said.
        </p>

        <p>
          "What did he say happened on the screens?" I asked.
        </p>

        <p>
          "All he ever said was that it began with random static and progressed to something like
          a language," James said. "Could it be the computer learning on its own? Like artificial
          intelligence? But now that I think about it, he mentioned the possibility of Russians
          jamming the signals."
        </p>

        <p>
          "Let me look through this material, James — I'm not sure the Russians would jam radar
          signals in order to cause symbols on the screens," I said. As he left, James said, "Oh,
          and this is weird, but once he said something about another purpose for the SAGE system.
          My grandfather mentioned something about using the radar systems to watch outer space."
        </p>

        <h2>The Projection System</h2>
        <p>
          The film strips of images that were displayed on the large command center screen had
          deteriorated, along with the magnetic and paper tape — but there were fragments that
          could be pieced together. Most of the film was the standard coastline imagery, with
          aircraft markers moving frame by frame.
        </p>

        <figure>
          <img src="/images/projectionSystem.png" alt="Basic principles of the SAGE Photographic Recorder-Reproducer (PRR) — projection mirror, film gate, feed and take-up spools" />
          <figcaption>Basic principles of the PRR — the projection system that put the picture on the command center screen</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <p>
          The projection system could take a picture of a CRT, process the image every 30 seconds,
          and then display it on the command center screen.
        </p>

        <p>
          Another source of film came from the Situation Display Camera Console — a normal SAGE
          display console with a self-advancing film camera strapped to it.
        </p>

        <figure>
          <img src="/images/computerStationCamera.png" alt="Situation Display Camera Console — a self-advancing film camera mounted to a SAGE display console" />
          <figcaption>Situation Display Camera Console</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <p>
          But there were a few interesting fragments in the film strips — pictures of symbols that
          looked almost modern.
        </p>

        <p>
          Using the machine-learning processes DCGAN and pix2pix, I was able to recreate the missing
          parts of the frames, and some of the missing frames themselves.
        </p>

        <p>
          The images make me really question what they saw that night. They allege an advanced
          intelligence that, in 1966, would not have been understood. There is a message here that
          could only be understood today. The symbols predict the future. The future that is now.
        </p>

        <p>
          <a href="/story/the-hypothesis">Read part two: The SAGE Hypothesis →</a>
        </p>

        <p>
          Shortly after this was first written, the first piece in a series of art generated from
          SAGE using AI and machine learning was released on
          <a href="https://superrare.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">SuperRare</a> — a
          marketplace for digital art that registers ownership to a set number of purchasers on a
          blockchain, a computer ledger stored on thousands of computers around the world. Owning
          the piece doesn't restrict who can view it, but it does mean you can resell it as its
          value changes.
        </p>

